  "fallbacks": [
    "Each shipped family is followed by metric-matched fallback faces over local system fonts (size-adjust and ascent, descent, and line-gap overrides), then a system stack and a generic family.",
    "There's a fallback face at each weight the roles set, over the system bold for a heading, and Fraunces Italic falls back to the system italic that fits it.",
    "Glyphs outside every shipped face's unicode-range render in the declared system stack, so text never goes missing."
  ],
  "caveats": [
    "Fraunces ships at its default SOFT 0 and WONK 0, since the full-axis file is over the ceiling on its own, in two faces: 600 upright and 400 italic.",
    "Captions and quotes set in \"Fraunces Italic\", the 400 italic under its own family name, so they render italic under every composition and never take the headings' 600.",
    "Inter ships upright only, at its wght axis without optical size, so em in running text renders as a slanted Inter.",
    "The latin-ext subsets, 123,348 B together, load only on a page whose text needs them, such as ő or ł; a Latin page loads 89,204 B. Other scripts render in the declared system stack."
  ],
